<!-- std.fmt.md -->
# `std.fmt`

`std.fmt` (11): formatting. The traits `Display` (what `{x}` writes)
and `Debug` (what `{x:?}` writes), the `Formatter` they write into and
the `Spec` of a placeholder (width, fill, alignment, precision) are the
prelude's, which interpolation uses, and are reached from here too
(`from std.fmt import Display`).

## Re-exports

- `Display`: `std.core.Display`
- `Debug`: `std.core.Debug`
- `Formatter`: `std.core.Formatter`
- `Spec`: `std.core.Spec`
<!-- std.string.md -->
# `std.string`

`std.string` (11): text. A `String` is its UTF-8 bytes, `str` is the
type of a text literal, and `char` is a Unicode scalar value; their
methods (`len`, `chars`, `split`, `trim`, `find`, `replace`,
`to_lowercase` and `to_uppercase`, which map case over all of Unicode,
...) are the prelude's, and `String` is reached from here too (`from
std.string import String`; `str` and `char` are built-in names).
`Builder` makes a text from many pieces. Grapheme iteration and the
normalisation forms need Unicode's tables, which are not here yet.

## Re-exports

- `String`: `std.core.String`

## Contents

- [`Builder`](#Builder): A text made from many pieces: each `push` keeps its piece, and `build` copies them all into one `String` once, so making a long text from short pieces copies each byte once.
- [`Normalize`](#Normalize): Unicode's four normalisation forms of a text (11, "normalisation forms"; Unicode's UAX #15): `nfd` decomposes each character canonically and puts its marks in their canonical order, `nfc` composes the result again, and `nfkd` and `nfkc` do the same with the compatibility decompositions besides (`ﬁ` is `fi`, `²` is `2`). Two texts that read the same have the same `nfc`, and the same `nfkc` when they differ only in such forms.
- [`Graphemes`](#Graphemes): The graphemes of a text, what a reader takes for one character each (03: "`s.graphemes()` ... graphemes one method away"; Unicode's UAX #29), by the rules these tables carry: a base with the marks after it (and the variation selectors, the emoji modifiers and the tag characters), `\r\n`, a Hangul syllable written in jamo, two regional indicators (a flag), and a sequence joined by U+200D, each one grapheme; a control character is one of its own. The full rules would also keep a prepended character with what follows it (`Prepend`), join an Indic conjunct across its virama, and join after U+200D only between pictographs.
- [`breaks_between`](#breaks_between): Whether a grapheme ends between `p` and `c`, `flags` being the regional indicators of the grapheme so far.
- [`is_control`](#is_control)
- [`is_regional`](#is_regional)
- [`extends`](#extends): What a grapheme keeps after its base: a mark, a variation selector, an emoji modifier, a tag character.
- [`jamo_l`](#jamo_l)
- [`jamo`](#jamo): What `n` is of Hangul: a leading, a vowel or a final jamo, an open or a closed syllable, or none.
- [`JAMO_NONE`](#JAMO_NONE)
- [`JAMO_L`](#JAMO_L)
- [`JAMO_V`](#JAMO_V)
- [`JAMO_T`](#JAMO_T)
- [`JAMO_LV`](#JAMO_LV)
- [`JAMO_LVT`](#JAMO_LVT)
- [`HANGUL_BASE`](#HANGUL_BASE)
- [`HANGUL_L`](#HANGUL_L)
- [`HANGUL_V`](#HANGUL_V)
- [`HANGUL_T`](#HANGUL_T)
- [`HANGUL_VOWELS`](#HANGUL_VOWELS)
- [`HANGUL_FINALS`](#HANGUL_FINALS)
- [`HANGUL_COUNT`](#HANGUL_COUNT)
- [`is_hangul_syllable`](#is_hangul_syllable)
- [`normalized`](#normalized): The text `s` decomposed, compatibly with `compat`, its marks put in canonical order, and composed again with `compose`.
- [`decompose`](#decompose): The full decomposition of `n`, pushed onto `out`.
- [`pool_at`](#pool_at): The `k`th code point of the pool, six digits each, 24 to a line.
- [`class_of`](#class_of): The canonical combining class of `n`.
- [`reorder`](#reorder): Each run of marks put in canonical order: by class, the marks of one class kept in the order written.
- [`composed`](#composed): The canonical composition of a decomposed, ordered text.
- [`composition`](#composition): The character `a` and `b` compose, if any.
- [`record_find`](#record_find): The start of the record of `table` (records of `size` bytes, each with one separator after it) whose first field is `n`.
- [`record_range`](#record_range): The start of the record of `table` whose range, its first two fields, holds `n`.
- [`hex_at`](#hex_at): The number written in `width` hexadecimal digits from `at`.
- [`canonical_table`](#canonical_table): `CCCCCC-AAAAAA-BBBBBB-F`: the canonical decomposition of `C` into `A` and `B` (0 for none), but for the Hangul syllables, which are computed; `F` is 1 where the composition forms compose it again (2061 records).
- [`compositions_table`](#compositions_table): `AAAAAA-BBBBBB-CCCCCC`: the character `C` that `A` and `B` compose, sorted by `A`, then `B` (941 records).
- [`compatibility_table`](#compatibility_table): `CCCCCC-OOOO-LL`: the compatibility decomposition of `C`, the `L` code points of `compatibility_pool()` from the `O`th (3796 records).
- [`compatibility_pool`](#compatibility_pool): The code points of the compatibility decompositions, six hexadecimal digits each, 24 to a line (5576 code points).
- [`classes_table`](#classes_table): `LLLLLL-HHHHHH-CC`: the canonical combining class of the code points from `L` to `H`, for every class but 0 (388 records).
- [`marks_table`](#marks_table): `LLLLLL-HHHHHH`: the marks (`Mn`, `Mc` and `Me`) from `L` to `H`, which a grapheme keeps after its base (310 records).

<a id="Builder"></a>
## `Builder`

```sisu
pub struct Builder
    pieces: list[String]
    bytes: int
```

A text made from many pieces: each `push` keeps its piece, and `build`
copies them all into one `String` once, so making a long text from
short pieces copies each byte once.

```sisu
var b = Builder.new()
b.push("one")
b.push_char(',')
b.push(" two")
b.push_line("")
b.push_line("three")
assert b.build() == "one, two\nthree\n"
assert b.byte_len() == 15
```

<a id="Builder.new"></a>
### `Builder.new`

```sisu
pub pure def new() -> Builder
```

An empty builder.

