<a id="canonical_table"></a>
## `canonical_table`

```sisu
pure def canonical_table() -> str
```

`CCCCCC-AAAAAA-BBBBBB-F`: the canonical decomposition of `C` into `A` and
`B` (0 for none), but for the Hangul syllables, which are computed; `F` is
1 where the composition forms compose it again (2061 records).

<a id="compositions_table"></a>
## `compositions_table`

```sisu
pure def compositions_table() -> str
```

`AAAAAA-BBBBBB-CCCCCC`: the character `C` that `A` and `B` compose, sorted
by `A`, then `B` (941 records).

<a id="compatibility_table"></a>
## `compatibility_table`

```sisu
pure def compatibility_table() -> str
```

`CCCCCC-OOOO-LL`: the compatibility decomposition of `C`, the `L` code
points of `compatibility_pool()` from the `O`th (3796 records).

<a id="compatibility_pool"></a>
## `compatibility_pool`

```sisu
pure def compatibility_pool() -> str
```

The code points of the compatibility decompositions, six hexadecimal
digits each, 24 to a line (5576 code points).

<a id="classes_table"></a>
## `classes_table`

```sisu
pure def classes_table() -> str
```

`LLLLLL-HHHHHH-CC`: the canonical combining class of the code points from
`L` to `H`, for every class but 0 (388 records).

<a id="marks_table"></a>
## `marks_table`

```sisu
pure def marks_table() -> str
```

`LLLLLL-HHHHHH`: the marks (`Mn`, `Mc` and `Me`) from `L` to `H`, which a
grapheme keeps after its base (310 records).

## Implementations

```sisu
impl Normalize for str
impl Graphemes for str
```
<!-- std.time.md -->
# `std.time`

`std.time` (11): `DateTime`, `Duration`, `Timer`, `TimeError` and the
test clock `TestClock` are the prelude's, which this module also names;
`Ticker` and `Timeline` (N-35, D-075) are written here, over a `Clock`,
so the same code keeps real time in production and is deterministic
under the test clock.

## Re-exports

- `DateTime`: `std.core.DateTime`
- `Duration`: `std.core.Duration`
- `Timer`: `std.core.Timer`
- `TimeError`: `std.core.TimeError`
- `TestClock`: `std.core.TestClock`
- `Clock`: `std.core.Clock`

## Contents

- [`Ticker`](#Ticker): A periodic ticker that does not drift (N-35): its `n`-th tick is at `start + n * period` whatever each iteration took, so a slow iteration does not push the ticks after it back. A tick that passes while the loop is busy is skipped, not fired late, and counted in `missed`.
- [`Timeline`](#Timeline): Virtual time (Sonic Pi's model, N-35): `sleep(d)` moves the timeline's own time on by exactly `d` and then waits for the clock to reach it, so the time spent between sleeps does not accumulate as drift.
- [`Instant`](#Instant): A point on a clock's time line, to measure how long something took (11, `std.time`): `Instant.now(clock)` reads the clock, `elapsed` is the time since, and the difference of two is a `Duration`; an instant is ordered but is not a civil time, and shows as its nanoseconds on its clock. Its readings are the clock's monotonic ones (`ClockOps.monotonic`, decision 643): on the host's clock the time since the run's first reading, which a change of the system's time does not move, and on the test clock its time, so that the test clock makes them deterministic; a recorded run replays them. Two instants of different clocks do not compare.
- [`ZONES`](#ZONES): The zones whose rules `Zone.named` knows, each name with its rule as POSIX writes a `TZ` variable: the abbreviation and the offset west of UTC of its standard time, and of its daylight time with the rules of its start and its end (`M` month `.` week `.` weekday, `/` the local time), as the zones' rules stand since 2007 (decision 627).
- [`Change`](#Change): A day a zone's rule changes its offset on: the `week`-th `weekday` (0 for Sunday) of `month`, week 5 being the last, at `seconds` of local time.
- [`Zone`](#Zone): A time zone (11, `std.time`): the offset from UTC of its standard time and, when it has one, of its daylight time with the days that start and end it, each with the abbreviation it is shown with. `Zone.named` gives the zones of the table above by their names in the tz database; a time before the rules came into force is given today's rules (decision 627).
- [`change_seconds`](#change_seconds): The seconds since the epoch, as if UTC, of the local time a change gives in `year`.
- [`offset_text`](#offset_text): An offset east of UTC as RFC 3339 writes it: `+05:30`, `-03:00`.
- [`parse_rule`](#parse_rule): A rule as POSIX writes a `TZ` variable (the table's), or `None`.
- [`rule_name`](#rule_name): An abbreviation from `at`: letters, or any text inside `<` and `>`; and where it ends.
- [`rule_seconds`](#rule_seconds): A signed `hh[:mm[:ss]]` from `at`, in seconds; and where it ends.
- [`rule_change`](#rule_change): A change `Mm.w.d[/time]` from `at`, its time 02:00 when it gives none; and where it ends.

<a id="Ticker"></a>
## `Ticker`

```sisu
pub struct Ticker
    clock: Clock
    period: Duration
    next: DateTime
    ## The ticks skipped because they passed while the loop was busy.
    pub missed: int
```

A periodic ticker that does not drift (N-35): its `n`-th tick is at
`start + n * period` whatever each iteration took, so a slow iteration
does not push the ticks after it back. A tick that passes while the
loop is busy is skipped, not fired late, and counted in `missed`.

Each `wait` is a boundary where a running program adopts the newest
epoch of hot replacement (10, "Debugging and profiling": `Ticker.wait`
calls `live.boundary()`).

```sisu
let clock = Clock.frozen_at(DateTime.parse("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z").expect("a time"))
let start = clock.now()
var t = Ticker.every(clock, Duration.seconds(1))
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
assert clock.now() - start == Duration.seconds(2)
clock.advance(Duration.millis(3500))
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
assert clock.now() - start == Duration.seconds(6)
assert t.missed == 3
```

<a id="Ticker.every"></a>
### `Ticker.every`

```sisu
pub def every(clock: Clock, period: Duration) -> Ticker
    requires period > Duration.seconds(0)
```

Ticks every `period`, the first a period from now.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Ticker.wait"></a>
### `Ticker.wait`

```sisu
pub def wait(mut self) -> ! TaskError
```

Waits for the next tick still ahead, skipping those that passed.

Capabilities: `self` (`Clock`).

<a id="Timeline"></a>
## `Timeline`

```sisu
pub struct Timeline
    clock: Clock
    ## The timeline's time: where it is in its score.
    pub now: DateTime
```

Virtual time (Sonic Pi's model, N-35): `sleep(d)` moves the timeline's
own time on by exactly `d` and then waits for the clock to reach it, so
the time spent between sleeps does not accumulate as drift.

```sisu
let clock = Clock.frozen_at(DateTime.parse("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z").expect("a time"))
var tl = Timeline.start(clock)
let t0 = tl.now
tl.sleep(Duration.millis(250)).expect("not cancelled")
clock.advance(Duration.millis(100))
tl.sleep(Duration.millis(250)).expect("not cancelled")
assert tl.now - t0 == Duration.millis(500)
assert clock.now() - t0 == Duration.millis(500)
```

<a id="Timeline.start"></a>
### `Timeline.start`

```sisu
pub def start(clock: Clock) -> Timeline
```

A timeline whose time starts at the clock's.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Timeline.sleep"></a>
### `Timeline.sleep`

```sisu
pub def sleep(mut self, d: Duration) -> ! TaskError
```

Moves the timeline on by `d`, and waits until the clock is there.

Capabilities: `self` (`Clock`).

<a id="Instant"></a>
## `Instant`

```sisu
@derive(Copy, Eq, Ord, Hash, Debug)
pub struct Instant
    nanos: int

impl Add[Duration] for Instant
impl Sub[Duration] for Instant
impl Display for Instant
```

A point on a clock's time line, to measure how long something took (11,
`std.time`): `Instant.now(clock)` reads the clock, `elapsed` is the time
since, and the difference of two is a `Duration`; an instant is ordered
but is not a civil time, and shows as its nanoseconds on its clock. Its
readings are the clock's monotonic ones (`ClockOps.monotonic`, decision
643): on the host's clock the time since the run's first reading, which
a change of the system's time does not move, and on the test clock its
time, so that the test clock makes them deterministic; a recorded run
replays them. Two instants of different clocks do not compare.

```sisu
let clock = Clock.frozen_at(DateTime.parse("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z").expect("a time"))
let start = Instant.now(clock)
clock.advance(Duration.millis(1500))
assert start.elapsed(clock) == Duration.millis(1500)
let later = Instant.now(clock)
assert later > start and later.since(start) == Duration.millis(1500)
assert start + Duration.seconds(2) > later
```

<a id="Instant.now"></a>
### `Instant.now`

```sisu
pub def now(clock: Clock) -> Instant
```

The clock's monotonic reading now.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Instant.elapsed"></a>
### `Instant.elapsed`

```sisu
pub def elapsed(self, clock: Clock) -> Duration
```

The time from this instant to the clock's time now.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Instant.since"></a>
### `Instant.since`

```sisu
pub pure def since(self, earlier: Instant) -> Duration
```

The time from `earlier` to this instant, negative when `earlier` is
the later one.

<a id="ZONES"></a>
## `ZONES`

```sisu
const ZONES: list[(str, str)]
```

The zones whose rules `Zone.named` knows, each name with its rule as
POSIX writes a `TZ` variable: the abbreviation and the offset west of
UTC of its standard time, and of its daylight time with the rules of
its start and its end (`M` month `.` week `.` weekday, `/` the local
time), as the zones' rules stand since 2007 (decision 627).

<a id="Change"></a>
## `Change`

```sisu
@derive(Copy, Eq, Debug)
struct Change
    month: int
    week: int
    weekday: int
    seconds: int
```

A day a zone's rule changes its offset on: the `week`-th `weekday` (0 for
Sunday) of `month`, week 5 being the last, at `seconds` of local time.

<a id="Zone"></a>
## `Zone`

```sisu
@derive(Eq)
pub struct Zone
    ## The zone's name, as `Europe/Helsinki`, or `UTC+05:30` for a fixed one.
    pub name: String
    standard: String
    standard_offset: int
    daylight: String
    daylight_offset: int
    rules: (Change, Change)?

## A zone as the code that makes it writes it (decision 684):
## `Zone.named("Europe/Helsinki")`, `Zone.utc()` or `Zone.fixed(19800)`,
## and not its private fields, its rules among them.
impl Debug for Zone
```

A time zone (11, `std.time`): the offset from UTC of its standard time
and, when it has one, of its daylight time with the days that start and
end it, each with the abbreviation it is shown with. `Zone.named` gives
the zones of the table above by their names in the tz database; a time
before the rules came into force is given today's rules (decision 627).

```sisu
let helsinki = Zone.named("Europe/Helsinki").expect("a zone")
let summer = DateTime.parse("2026-07-01T12:00:00Z").expect("a time")
let winter = DateTime.parse("2026-01-15T12:00:00Z").expect("a time")
assert helsinki.offset_at(summer) == Duration.hours(3)
assert helsinki.abbreviation_at(winter) == "EET"
assert helsinki.format(summer) == "2026-07-01T15:00:00+03:00"
assert helsinki.local(summer).hour() == 15
assert Zone.named("Mars/Olympus").is_err()
```

<a id="Zone.utc"></a>
### `Zone.utc`

```sisu
pub pure def utc() -> Zone
```

UTC itself.

<a id="Zone.fixed"></a>
### `Zone.fixed`

```sisu
pub pure def fixed(seconds: int) -> Zone
```

A zone of one offset, `seconds` east of UTC, named `UTC+HH:MM`.

<a id="Zone.named"></a>
### `Zone.named`

```sisu
pub pure def named(name: str) -> Zone ! TimeError
```

The zone named `name` in the tz database, of the ones the table
knows; `InvalidTime` for another name.

<a id="Zone.offset_at"></a>
### `Zone.offset_at`

```sisu
pub pure def offset_at(self, t: DateTime) -> Duration
```

The offset from UTC the zone's clocks show at `t`.

<a id="Zone.abbreviation_at"></a>
### `Zone.abbreviation_at`

```sisu
pub pure def abbreviation_at(self, t: DateTime) -> String
```

The abbreviation the zone's time is shown with at `t`, as `EEST`.

<a id="Zone.local"></a>
### `Zone.local`

```sisu
pub pure def local(self, t: DateTime) -> DateTime
```

The zone's wall-clock time at `t`, as a `DateTime` whose parts
(`hour`, `day`, ...) are the local ones: `t` moved by its offset.

<a id="Zone.format"></a>
### `Zone.format`

```sisu
pub pure def format(self, t: DateTime) -> String
```

`t` as RFC 3339 writes it in the zone, its local time and offset:
`2026-07-01T15:00:00+03:00`.

<a id="Zone.seconds_at"></a>
### `Zone.seconds_at`

```sisu
pure def seconds_at(self, t: DateTime) -> int
```

The offset at `t`, in seconds east of UTC.

<a id="Zone.daylight_at"></a>
### `Zone.daylight_at`

```sisu
pure def daylight_at(self, t: DateTime) -> bool
```

Whether daylight time is in force at `t`: from its start, a local
standard time, to its end, a local daylight time, of the year `t`
falls in locally; the end comes first in a year of the southern
hemisphere.

